Complexity is certainly an issue. I have looked at this particular 
problem off and on for a couple years now and have not gotten much 
farther than a couple half hearted attempts to handle how to start the 
program / recognize when Nethack is waiting for input. As an example, 
RogOMatic generally waits until the cursor waits on the @ symbol. For 
Nethack, that only handles about 60% of the typical inputs you make. 
Until this is solved - the rest of the problem is pretty moot (unless 
you build the bot into Nethack).

About the strategy vs. tactics concerns raised by David, there are some 
methods from simulated autonomous forces (SAFOR) that I would adapt. 
Let's do a simple example of a tank moving from point A to B across a 
field with a few buildings in the way. An old model would literally have 
the tank take the direct path and eventually all the buildings between A 
and B would be flattened. A more recent model used a direction vector 
with a number of factors that would effect the direction. A "avoid 
collision" rule can have a inverse squared (or cubed) relationship to 
force the vector away from buildings. The "go to destination" rule would 
simply point to the destination. The combination of these (and several 
other rules) results [and has been demonstrated] in relatively smooth 
motion that approximates human behavior. You would have a set of these - 
for example, you have to feel "safe" (almost no danger) before trying to 
identify / consolidate items. These can be hard to tune, but the bot had 
better adjust its parameters automatically.

The other way I would help limit the number of strategic / tactical 
decisions is to break the problem down into subgoals. From my guide, 
there are only a handful of actions you have to do - that would be a 
starting point. For example, Sokoban is optional - I might avoid trying 
to solve that with a bot until it could regularly get to the castle. 
Actually - that would be a good demonstration as an interim example - 
get to the castle and escape with the wand of wishing. That would at 
least be at a level of effort similar to ROM.


